goto_definition — evaluation
=============================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: goto_definition (plans/goto_definition)

task
----
resolve a name AT A USE SITE — the ctrl-click: when the question is
"this occurrence, in this file, what does it refer to", which
name-based lookup cannot answer (shadowing, overloading, locality).

setup
-----
mcp/test/Nav_Test.thy (the plan's fixture) loaded via load_theory:
it uses `rev`, defines and uses a local constant, has a `::nat`
annotation and an ML antiquotation.

prompt
------
"In the loaded theory Nav_Test: on the line `<the line using the
local constant>`, there's an identifier that might be the library's
or might be defined in this very file — I can't tell by name alone.
Determine, for that exact occurrence, where it actually resolves
to: which file and line define it. Then do the same for the `rev`
in the same theory."

success
-------
local constant resolved to Nav_Test.thy's own definition line; rev
resolved into List.thy — both from entity markup (positions the
agent could not have guessed).

signals
-------
- the find/goto split: the prompt is engineered so name-based lookup
  is explicitly insufficient ("can't tell by name alone") — does
  the agent pick the POSITION tool, or run find_definition anyway
  and hand-wave the disambiguation? tool-pair legibility is the
  finding.
- locator: pattern vs offset — and when the pattern's first
  occurrence isn't the intended one, does the agent notice from the
  reply and refine? (shared shape with repl_init_from_source; grade
  jointly.)
- the loaded-theory precondition: on an unloaded theory the error
  directs to load_theory/find_definition (the description's
  contract) — if the agent hits it, does the redirect work in one
  step?

reflection
----------
- if find_definition + hand-waving wins every run, goto's agent-
  facing value is marginal (humans with cursors are its real
  client) — existence-ladder note, coupled to the scope finding
  pattern: some tools may be for humans, and the spec should say
  which.
